.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt INIT 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm init
.Nd process control initialization
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl R
.Op Fl s
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
program is the first process the kernel starts, with process ID 1.
It runs the boot script, starts and supervises the login sessions on
the terminals, collects every orphaned process, and shuts the system
down when asked.
It has no controlling terminal: each session it starts is a session of
its own, so that signals from the keyboard never reach
.Nm .
.Pp
The kernel passes its boot flags as arguments:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl s
Start in single-user mode.
.It Fl R
Recovery: the kernel restarted
.Nm
after it died.
.Nm
does not run
.Pa /etc/rc
again.
It logs why it was restarted, starts each enabled service with the
.Dq shutdown
keyword (those
.Pa /etc/rc.shutdown
stops) that is not running, keeps the sessions that are still running,
and starts the missing ones.
.El
.Ss States
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It single-user
A root shell runs on the console.
If
.Xr ttys 5
marks the
.Dq console
entry
.Dq insecure ,
root's password is asked for first;
typing
.Ql ^D
at the prompt skips the shell.
When the shell exits,
.Nm
goes on to run
.Pa /etc/rc ,
or, if single-user mode was entered from multi-user mode, returns to
multi-user mode directly.
.It runcom
.Pa /etc/rc
is run with
.Pa /sbin/init_sh ,
with the argument
.Dq autoboot
at boot only.
If it fails,
.Nm
enters single-user mode.
.It multi-user
For each entry of
.Xr ttys 5
that is on and has a command,
.Nm
runs the command with the terminal's name appended, and
.Ev TERM
set to the entry's type, and starts it again whenever it exits.
An entry whose command exits within 5 seconds of starting 3 times in a
row is not started again for 30 seconds.
.El
.Ss Signals
.Bl -tag -width SIGUSR1
.It Dv SIGHUP
Read
.Xr ttys 5
again: start sessions for new entries and hang up those whose entry
was removed, turned off, or given a different command or type.
Also undoes
.Dv SIGTSTP .
.It Dv SIGTERM
Hang up every session and enter single-user mode.
Services keep running.
.It Dv SIGTSTP
Start no new sessions; those running continue.
.It Dv SIGINT
Shut down and reboot.
.It Dv SIGUSR1
Shut down and halt.
.It Dv SIGUSR2
Shut down and power off.
.El
.Pp
To shut down,
.Nm
hangs up every session, runs
.Pa /etc/rc.shutdown
(terminating it after 90 seconds), sends
.Dv SIGTERM
to every process, then
.Dv SIGKILL
to any still there after 5 seconds, synchronizes the file systems, and
calls
.Xr reboot 2 .
.Xr reboot 8 ,
.Xr halt 8 ,
.Xr poweroff 8
and
.Xr shutdown 8
send these signals.
.Ss Logging
.Nm
logs its messages with
.Xr syslog 3
to the
.Dq auth
facility; until
.Xr syslogd 8
is running they go to the console.
After the first successful
.Pa /etc/rc
it writes a
.Dv BOOT_TIME
record to
.Xr utmpx 5
and
.Pa /var/log/wtmpx ,
and when shutting down, a
.Dv SHUTDOWN_TIME
record.
When a session ends while its login record still shows a user logged in,
.Nm
closes the record with
.Dv DEAD_PROCESS .
.Ss Death of init
If
.Nm
dies, the kernel records why in
.Pa /proc/initdeaths
and starts it again with
.Fl R .
After three deaths within 30 seconds it runs
.Xr emergency 8
instead.
Writing a signal number to the
.Va debug.kill_init
.Xr sysctl 8
variable kills
.Nm
with that signal, to test this.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Nm
ignores its own environment.
Its children get
.Ev PATH
.Pa ( /sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin ) ,
.Ev HOME
.Pa ( / ) ,
.Ev SHELL
.Pa ( /bin/sh )
and
.Ev TERM .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/rc.shutdown -compact
.It Pa /dev/console
the console
.It Pa /etc/rc
the boot script
.It Pa /etc/rc.shutdown
the shutdown script
.It Pa /etc/ttys
the terminals to run sessions on
.It Pa /proc/initdeaths
the kernel's record of deaths of
.Nm
.It Pa /var/run/utmpx
current sessions, and the last boot and shutdown
.It Pa /var/log/wtmpx
the history of logins, boots and shutdowns
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr syslog 3 ,
.Xr ttys 5 ,
.Xr utmpx 5 ,
.Xr emergency 8 ,
.Xr getty 8 ,
.Xr rc 8 ,
.Xr reboot 8 ,
.Xr shutdown 8 ,
.Xr syslogd 8
.Sh HISTORY
An
.Nm
program appeared in
.At v1 .
This implementation was written for OxideBSD; its states and signals
are those of
.Fx .
